  "notes": "London's bedroom-jazz-guitar wing — \"boom-bap drums under jazz-funk guitar\". The drum block holds the entry's two options in one kit: \"a boom-bap-lineage programmed loop — a sampled or sampled-sounding kick and snare with the backbeat hard on 2&4, swung 16th hats, vinyl noise and MPC-style timing offsets; on the band material a live kit replaces it with heavy ghost-note density and displaced snares\". The MPC offsets and the live displacement together are why `quantizeStrength` sits at 0.6 with a 5–13 ms snare window and a 0.56 ghost probability across five positions — programmed, but not on the grid. Harmony is the entry's most-moving in this batch: \"maj7/m7/9 voicings, ii–V–I turnarounds, modal interchange, and 4-to-8-bar cycles with genuine motion. Chord changes are frequent by lane standards\", so the chord durations are 2–6 beats and the harmonic rhythm is one-per-bar and syncopated-cell rather than vamped. The lead voice is not the singer: \"clean or lightly-compressed electric guitar is the lead voice — chord-melody comping, single-note funk lines and long improvised solos… Vocals are soft, thin mid-tenor at low note-density and are frequently secondary to the guitar; instrumental tracks are common\". That is a low melodic density with a raised `emptyBarBias`, against the densest countermelody of any artist in this batch (0.7) carrying the guitar. Bass is \"funk-syncopated 16ths with slides and octave leaps, mixed forward. He plays bass and guitar himself.\" ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are all drum probabilities and velocities and every melody number, plus the second progression family — the entry names \"modal interchange\", and the borrowed I–iv is this author's reading of that phrase.",
